\begin{abstract}
Execution-free verification for LLM-based code repair can be formulated as per-test binary classification (\cls{}), execution output generation (\cwm{}), or trajectory-level patch scoring. No prior work compares these formulations under matched conditions. We present a systematic comparison across two model families, two complexity levels, and multiple seeds under ${\leq}$8B LoRA fine-tuning.

At function level, all three formulations achieve near-equivalent accuracy (TOST equivalence within $\pm$2\pp{}, 5 seeds). At repository level, divergence amplifies to 19--26\pp{}, with generation-based simulation falling below majority baseline. The pattern is consistent across model families but model-contingent in magnitude.

Per-test classification provides diagnostic granularity at comparable accuracy, with configuration-dependent aggregation cost at repository level. Formulations exhibit complementary errors at function level, motivating complexity-aware routing over uniform formulation selection.
\end{abstract}

tool_args.new_string:
\begin{abstract}
LLM-based program repair increasingly relies on generating multiple candidate patches and selecting the best via a verifier. Execution-based verification is reliable but expensive; three execution-free alternatives have emerged: per-test binary classification (\cls{}), execution output generation (\cwm{}), and trajectory-level patch scoring (\traj{}). No prior work compares these formulations under matched conditions.

We present the first systematic comparison across two model families (Qwen3, DeepSeek-Coder), two complexity levels, and multiple seeds under ${\leq}$8B LoRA fine-tuning. Our primary finding is that formulation choice does not matter at function level: all three achieve statistically equivalent accuracy (TOST $p{=}0.007$, $\pm$2\pp{} margin, 5 seeds, Holm-Bonferroni corrected), establishing that classification is not inherently easier than generation when outputs are short. At repository level, preliminary single-seed evidence suggests sharp divergence (19--26\pp{}), with generation-based simulation falling below majority baseline under our protocol; multiple identified confounds limit causal attribution.

Error complementarity across formulations (oracle ensemble: 90.63\%) and a failure mode taxonomy motivate complexity-aware formulation routing over uniform selection.
\end{abstract}

